---
// Section root for residency.
//
// The subdomain opens with the Golden Visa, because that is what people
// search for. This page opens with the rule that decides whether any
// residence visa survives at all — the 180 days — because that is the one
// every route shares and the one nobody reads until it has bitten them.
import VerticalLayout from "../../layouts/VerticalLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "@dst/ui/PageHero.astro";
import Callout from "@dst/ui/Callout.astro";
import Faq from "@dst/ui/Faq.astro";
import { verticalBySlug } from "../../verticals";

const v = verticalBySlug("visas")!;

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: "How long is a UAE residence visa valid?",
    a: "It depends on the type and the sponsor: one, two or three years on a sponsored visa, and up to five or ten years on an unsponsored one. A dependent's visa never outlasts the sponsor's.",
  },
  {
    q: "What happens if I leave the UAE for a long stretch?",
    a: "The general rule is that living outside the country for more than 180 continuous days nullifies the residence visa automatically, and a new entry permit is then needed. There is a list of exemptions, and Golden and Green residency holders are outside the rule entirely.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is there a fine for overstaying?",
    a: "AED 50 a day, standardised by the ICP across visit, tourist and residence visas. It was AED 25 for residence overstays before the change.",
  },
  {
    q: "Who renews a residence visa?",
    a: "The sponsor, before it expires. Residents are given flexible grace periods of up to six months, by category, to stay in the country after a permit is cancelled or expires — and when the visa expires so does the Emirates ID.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I cancel my own visa?",
    a: "Normally not. Only the sponsor can, either through an ICP-registered typing centre or online through the ICP website or app. Anyone sponsoring dependents must cancel theirs before cancelling their own.",
  },
];
---
<VerticalLayout
  vertical="visas"
  prose
  title="Dubai residency: the routes and what each one costs"
  description="Residence visas in Dubai run one to ten years depending on who sponsors you. The 180-day rule, the AED 50 daily overstay fine, and which routes sit outside both."
  image="/covers/visas-section.jpg"
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Residency and visas"
    title="Dubai residency: the routes and what each one costs"
    image="/covers/visas-section.jpg"
    imageAlt="Cut-paper illustration: a row of pale paper arches of differing heights standing on a slate-blue ground."
    imageKind="generated"
  >
    <p class="lede">
      Every residence visa in the UAE dies the same way. Live outside the
      country for more than <strong>180 continuous days</strong> and it is
      nullified automatically — no notice, no letter, and a new entry permit
      needed to come back. What differs between the routes is how long the
      visa runs, who has to sponsor it, and whether that 180-day rule applies
      to you at all.
    </p>
  </PageHero>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>One, two, three — or five and ten</h2>
    <p>
      Validity follows the type and the sponsor. A sponsored visa runs
      <strong>one, two or three years</strong>. An unsponsored one runs up to
      <strong>five or ten</strong>. A dependent's visa never expires later
      than the sponsor's, which is the quiet constraint behind every family
      calculation: the family's dates are the sponsor's dates.
    </p>
    <p>
      Above 18, a residence visa means a medical fitness test, a security
      check and an Emirates ID from the ICP. That is the floor, and it is the
      same floor whichever route brought you.
    </p>

    <h2>The 180 days, and who is outside them</h2>
    <p>
      The general rule nullifies a residence visa after more than 180
      continuous days abroad. The published exemptions are specific rather
      than general: the foreign wife of an Emirati citizen; residents sent
      abroad for treatment with an approved medical report; public sector
      employees sent for training or posted to an employer's office abroad,
      with their families; students enrolled in institutions abroad;
      investors holding valid residence visas; residents sponsored by UAE
      diplomatic representatives; and domestic helpers in several named
      situations.
    </p>
    <p>
      Anyone who has already fallen foul of it applies for an entry permit
      from outside the country, after the 180 days, with a reason — and pays
      <strong>AED 100 for every 30 days or less</strong> spent away. The
      approval then has to be used within 30 days. The service is not for
      residents of Dubai, and holders of Golden and Green residency do not
      need it: their residency lets them enter directly for as long as it is
      valid.
    </p>

    <h2>What each page here answers</h2>
    <ul>
      {v.pages.map((p) => (
        <li>
          <a href={`/visas/${p.path}/`} title={p.title}>{p.label}</a> — {p.title}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <Callout kind="warn">
    <p>
      Overstay fines were standardised by the ICP at <strong>AED 50 a
      day</strong> across visit, tourist and residence visas — double the
      previous AED 25 for a residence overstay. The visa's expiry is also the
      Emirates ID's expiry, so the document you would reach for to prove
      anything expires at the same moment.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <section class="container">
    <h2>Beyond the usual routes</h2>
    <p>
      Two long-term visas exist for reasons that have nothing to do with
      salary or property. The <strong>Blue visa</strong> is a ten-year
      residence visa for people who have made an exceptional contribution to
      environmental protection and sustainability, inside or outside the UAE
      — granted to Emiratis and residents alike, by application to the ICP or
      by nomination from a competent authority. The <strong>Green
      visa</strong> covers skilled workers, freelancers and the
      self-employed, and its holders may sponsor first-degree relatives.
    </p>
    <p>
      A residency guide with its own news feed sits at
      <a href={`https://${v.host}`} title="The residency and visas site">{v.host}</a>,
      and what is changing in Dubai generally is on
      <a href="/news/" title="What's changing in Dubai">the news page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Faq items={faqItems} />

  <section class="container">
    <p class="source-note">
      Validity, the 180-day rule and its exemptions, the entry permit and its
      AED 100 charge, the AED 50 overstay fine, renewal and cancellation: the
      Official Platform of the UAE Government, general provisions for the
      residence visa, citing the ICP. Blue visa and Green visa: the same
      platform, types of visas. Both read 15 September 2026. Conditions
      change; the ICP and the GDRFA are where a case is decided, not here.
    </p>
  </section>
</VerticalLayout>
